text:
liz: quick report - onboarding guide + sample app tripped a new user: deploying our example to a Vercel Edge function blew up with "fetch is not defined". looks like ESM bundle assumes global fetch.

omar: that makes sense. some edge runtimes (older workers/npm edge) have fetch, but some Node-based edge shims and older CF workers don't. our current package didn't include a runtime-safe fallback.

pat: TL;DR: undici is fine for Node but cannot be shipped to Workers. we need an edge-first ESM entry that never imports undici, and a Node-friendly CJS entry that can require undici.

liz: sketch of exports in package.json to avoid bundler confusion:

```
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/edge/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/node/index.cjs"
  }
}
```

that keeps the edge bundle tiny and avoids accidental undici inclusion.

omar: also recommend not doing top-level dynamic imports. If you must, do them inside a function and only when you detect environment looks like Node (process and !globalThis.WorkerGlobalScope). Example snippet for samples/docs:

```js
async function getFetch() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  // likely Node
  try {
    const { fetch } = await import('undici');
    return fetch;
  } catch (err) {
    throw new Error('No fetch available. Use edge-compatible build or provide a polyfill.');
  }
}
```

pat: remember to not call getFetch at module init for edge bundles. call inside init/handler so bundlers don't eagerly include undici.

roxie: typing footgun: when TS users have lib.dom in their tsconfig, they get DOM fetch types, but Node users often don't. we should ship a minimal index.d.ts that declares Request/Response types if the consumer doesn't include lib.dom. keep it tiny and defensive.

liz: agree. proposal: ship self-contained d.ts in dist/types/index.d.ts with conditional ambient defs. we can't do conditional types based on tsconfig, but we can provide our own minimal types to avoid build errors. add note in README: "If you rely on DOM types from TypeScript, keep lib.dom in tsconfig; otherwise our bundled types should suffice."

omar: CI: add jobs to run the sample app in three environments: Node 18 (native fetch), Node 16 (no fetch -> install undici), and a Workers runtime (miniflare). That will catch regressions.

ci-bot: queued CI changes: +job(samples:node18) +job(samples:node16-undici) +job(samples:miniflare). linked PR when ready.

pat: packaging: make undici a runtime dependency for the Node CJS build (not for the edge bundle). mark it in package.json under optionalDependencies? better in dependencies for CJS so require() resolution works. edge bundle should not list it.

roxie: docs/samples: add a short section "Edge vs Node: fetch guidance" with snippets for Next.js users: when targeting Edge Runtime use the ESM edge import; for API routes/serverless use node CJS and ensure Node 18+ or undici. also include the safe-getFetch snippet.

liz: also add a low-level telemetry event if we fall back to undici at runtime so we can measure how many customers actually need it. log at debug level and tie to feature flags.

omar: small runtime detail: in Node v18 global fetch exists but it's not fully spec-compatible in some versions. still ok for our use but mention in README: prefer Node 18.7+ or undici for consistent behavior.

pat: tests: unit test around loader logic to assert we don't attempt to import undici when a fake Worker global exists. use jsdom/miniflare mocks for that.

liz: action list: 1) eng: produce dual outputs (dist/edge/index.mjs + dist/node/index.cjs) and adjust exports (omar/pat), 2) typings: create self-contained index.d.ts that provides minimal Fetch shapes (roxie), 3) devex: update sample app and onboarding docs + add migration note for Next.js (liz), 4) ci: add three sample runtime jobs (ci-bot). target: this sprint.

pat: I'll open PR for build scripts and exports. will keep undici in node package deps.

omar: I'll write the safe-fetch pattern and add unit tests around environment detection.

roxie: I'll draft the d.ts shim and a small README paragraph about types.

